<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Frak wallet app detection — strictly scoped, never QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES
      (Play policy restricts it to apps whose core purpose needs broad
      visibility; requesting it drags every integrator into a Permissions
      Declaration review).

      Both variants are listed: the wallet ships as `id.frak.wallet` in prod and
      `id.frak.wallet.dev` for the dev stage (see
      `packages/wallet-shared/src/common/utils/storeUrls.ts`). Querying only the
      prod package makes a locally-built dev wallet undetectable — exactly the
      build a POC runs against.

      Shipping this from the SDK is the point (02 §4): the manifest merger folds
      it into every merchant app, so `AppInstalledProbe` works without the
      merchant knowing the wallet's package ids.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="id.frak.wallet" />
        <package android:name="id.frak.wallet.dev" />
    </queries>

    <!--
      The only permission the SDK will ever request. A library's manifest is
      merged into the host app, so anything added here is a permission the
      merchant did not ask for and has to justify on their store listing.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      No <application> block, and deliberately no exported activity and no
      intent filter.

      02 §5.5 forbids a redirect-catcher activity: inbound `fCtx` handling is
      wired through `FrakConfig.deepLink` (Automatic via
      ActivityLifecycleCallbacks covering cold *and* warm start, or Manual via
      `handleReferralLink(url)`), so the merchant's own activity keeps owning
      the intent. An SDK-declared filter would silently compete with the
      merchant's router for their own links, and an exported component is
      attack surface we would be adding to every integrator's app.

      `frak_data_extraction_rules.xml` is NOT referenced from here either: on
      `<application>`, `android:dataExtractionRules` is a singular attribute and
      the merger does not union it, so declaring it would either be dropped or
      break the build of any merchant with their own rules file, pending
      `tools:replace` (02 §3). It ships as a resource for merchants to reference
      or copy, plus a debug-build runtime assertion once there is code to assert.
    -->

</manifest>
